config CRUX_CONTROLLER_S1
	bool "Step S1: the workload on Zephyr threads and a message queue"
	depends on MULTITHREADING
	# The adapter zone builds absolute deadlines (K_TIMEOUT_ABS_TICKS), which need 64-bit kernel
	# timeouts. Zephyr turns them on by default, and the Crux Thread and Queue providers selected
	# them while the workload called those providers. The product asks for them itself now.
	select TIMEOUT_64BIT
	help
	  Run the workload: a producer and a consumer thread joined by one message queue, created by
	  the adapter zone app/os with Zephyr kernel calls, reading Crux Time and reporting through
	  Crux Logging. Steps S2 and S3 build on this one. The product uses no Crux Thread, Sync or
	  Queue provider, so none is selected for it.

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_S2
	bool "Step S2: send each workload message to a TCP peer"
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S1 && CRUX_SOCKETS && CRUX_NETIF
	help
	  After a message is logged, open a TCP connection to the peer, send the message
	  and expect the same eight bytes back. The Sockets profile of Crux defines the
	  calls that are used. The exchange is built and never run in Stage 1 CI.

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_PEER_ADDRESS
	string "IPv4 address of the TCP peer"
	default "192.168.77.1"
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S2

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_PEER_PORT
	int "TCP port of the peer"
	default 42420
	range 1 65535
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S2

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_S3
	bool "Step S3: keep the workload configuration record in a file"
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S1 && CRUX_FILE_FATFS_RT1060
	help
	  At start, mount the microSD volume that the devicetree entry controller_storage
	  describes, bind its files directory to Crux File, load the configuration record from
	  the file config.rec and use it, or save the default record when none exists. The image
	  creates no directory and never formats a volume: the directory "files" must already
	  exist on the card, and a card without it leaves the default configuration in use, as
	  does a volume that does not mount. Built and never run in Stage 1 CI.

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_S4
	bool "Step S4: report the reset cause and feed a watchdog from the workload cycle"
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S1 && CRUX_SYSTEM && WATCHDOG && HWINFO && REBOOT
	help
	  At start, write one line for the reset cause through Crux Logging and clear the flags, then
	  start the one watchdog of the boot with an interval of about ten workload cycles and feed it
	  once for each pass that has cycles due, from the workload's producer thread and nowhere else. The
	  application uses <crux/system.h> only. The native watchdog, HWINFO and reboot facilities,
	  and the devicetree alias crux-watchdog0 (boards/s4.overlay), are what Crux System requires of
	  the image; the product selects no Crux symbol. A platform without a watchdog, such as the
	  native_sim simulator, reports it as not supported and the workload runs without one. A missed
	  feed ends in the watchdog's own reset; the product adds none. Built and never run on a board.

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_S5
	bool "Step S5: set the Crux Time realtime clock from an SNTP server"
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S2 && CRUX_TIME && SNTP && NET_UDP && NET_IPV4
	help
	  Query one SNTP server at a fixed interval, from a low-priority thread, and use the answer to
	  set the Crux Time realtime clock (source EXTERNAL, synchronised) through the Crux Time
	  authority, <crux/time_authority.h>. The product's policy decides what an answer may do. Any
	  in-range answer sets a clock that is not valid or not synchronised. A synchronised clock
	  leaves a small difference alone, sets a difference up to the maximum step, and refuses a
	  larger one and marks synchronisation lost. A failed, timed-out or kiss-o'-death answer, or one
	  outside 2000-01-01 to 2100-01-01, never sets, and a clock that nothing confirms for the stale
	  age is marked not synchronised. The step is a jump of the realtime clock, not a slew. SNTP
	  here is unauthenticated: a server, or anything on the path, that answers with a time in range
	  can move the clock. The time is not persisted: there is no RTC, and after a reset it is not
	  valid until the first answer. The server is an IPv4 address because the image has no DNS
	  resolver. Built on the S2 network. Built and never run on a board.

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_TIME_SERVER
	string "IPv4 address of the SNTP server"
	default "192.168.77.1"
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S5
	help
	  An IPv4 address in dotted form, optionally followed by a colon and a port (123 otherwise).
	  The image has no DNS resolver, so a host name does not resolve.

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_TIME_INTERVAL_S
	int "Seconds between two queries"
	default 600
	range 16 86400
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S5
	help
	  The thread queries once, shortly after start, and then waits this long after each query. The
	  lower bound keeps clear of RFC 4330, which asks for no more than one query in 15 seconds.

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_TIME_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "Milliseconds to wait for the answer to one query"
	default 3000
	range 100 30000
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S5
	help
	  The Zephyr library resends the request with a growing wait until this time is over, so a
	  query can take about this long. It must be shorter than the interval.

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_TIME_STALE_AGE_S
	int "Seconds after which an unconfirmed synchronised clock is marked not synchronised"
	default 1800
	range 60 604800
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S5
	help
	  Counted from the last answer that set the clock or agreed with it, strictly: an age equal
	  to this value is not stale yet. It must be longer than the interval.

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_TIME_THRESHOLD_MS
	int "Milliseconds below which a difference from a synchronised clock is left alone"
	default 100
	range 1 10000
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S5
	help
	  A difference of exactly this size is corrected. It must not exceed the maximum step.

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_TIME_MAX_STEP_MS
	int "Milliseconds above which a difference from a synchronised clock is refused"
	default 1000
	range 1 3600000
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S5
	help
	  A difference of exactly this size is still corrected. A larger one is refused once and marks
	  synchronisation lost, so that the next in-range answer sets the clock whatever it says. The
	  limit therefore guards against one wrong answer, not against a server that keeps giving one.

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_TIME_STACK
	int "Stack bytes of the time synchronisation thread"
	default 8192
	range 2048 65536
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S5
	help
	  A starting value, not measured: it is the 8192 bytes that the S2 consumer thread uses for the
	  same reach into the network stack. Stack use of this thread has not been measured.

config CRUX_CONTROLLER_WORKER_STACK
	int "Stack bytes of each workload thread"
	default 4096
	range 1024 65536
	depends on CRUX_CONTROLLER_S1
	help
	  Each of the two workload threads has a stack of this size. The default is what the Crux
	  Thread provider gave the workload before it called Zephyr directly, and the S2 image sets
	  8192 because its exchange reaches into the network stack.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
